1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,153
Peter:

2
00:00:01,153 --> 00:00:01,400
Hello?

3
00:00:01,400 --> 00:00:01,701
Jan:

4
00:00:01,701 --> 00:00:02,163
Oh hello.

5
00:00:02,163 --> 00:00:03,911
My name's Jan.

6
00:00:03,911 --> 00:00:09,350
Are you the right person to talk to about the Buckworth Conservation Group?

7
00:00:09,350 --> 00:00:09,400
Peter:

8
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:10,699
Yes, I'm Peter.

9
00:00:10,699 --> 00:00:11,710
I'm the secretary.

10
00:00:11,710 --> 00:00:12,073
Jan:

11
00:00:12,073 --> 00:00:12,435
Good.

12
00:00:12,435 --> 00:00:14,611
I've just moved to this area,

13
00:00:14,611 --> 00:00:16,787
and I'm interested in getting involved.

14
00:00:16,787 --> 00:00:20,776
I was in a similar group where I used to live.

15
00:00:20,776 --> 00:00:24,040
Could you tell me something about your activities, please?

16
00:00:24,040 --> 00:00:24,329
Peter:

17
00:00:24,329 --> 00:00:24,906
Of course.

18
00:00:24,906 --> 00:00:28,082
Well, we have a mixture of regular activities and special events.

19
00:00:28,082 --> 00:00:32,124
One of the regular ones is trying to keep the beach free of litter.

20
00:00:32,124 --> 00:00:35,878
A few of us spend a couple of hours a month on it,

21
00:00:35,878 --> 00:00:38,477
and it's awful how much there is to clear.

22
00:00:38,477 --> 00:00:42,230
I wish people would be more responsible and take it home with them.

23
00:00:42,230 --> 00:00:42,603
Jan:

24
00:00:42,603 --> 00:00:43,722
I totally agree.

25
00:00:43,722 --> 00:00:46,332
I'd be happy to help with that.

26
00:00:46,332 --> 00:00:48,570
Is it OK to take dogs?

27
00:00:48,570 --> 00:00:48,881
Peter:

28
00:00:48,881 --> 00:00:51,990
I'm afraid not, as they're banned from the beach itself.

29
00:00:51,990 --> 00:00:54,477
You can take them along the cliffs, though.

30
00:00:54,477 --> 00:00:55,720
And children are welcome.

31
00:00:55,720 --> 00:00:56,390
Jan:

32
00:00:56,390 --> 00:00:57,060
Right.

33
00:00:57,060 --> 00:00:57,353
Peter:

34
00:00:57,353 --> 00:00:59,113
We also manage a nature reserve,

35
00:00:59,113 --> 00:01:02,047
and there's a lot to do there all year round.

36
00:01:02,047 --> 00:01:02,633
For example,

37
00:01:02,633 --> 00:01:07,620
because it's a popular place to visit, we spend a lot of time looking after the paths

38
00:01:07,620 --> 00:01:10,260
and making sure they're in good condition for walking.

39
00:01:10,260 --> 00:01:10,620
Jan:

40
00:01:10,620 --> 00:01:12,780
I could certainly help with that.

41
00:01:12,780 --> 00:01:13,166
Peter:

42
00:01:13,166 --> 00:01:13,553
Good.

43
00:01:13,553 --> 00:01:17,416
And we have a programme of creating new habitats there.

44
00:01:17,416 --> 00:01:22,052
We've just finished making and installing nesting boxes for birds to use,

45
00:01:22,052 --> 00:01:27,075
and next we're going to work on encouraging insects – they're important for the

46
00:01:27,075 --> 00:01:28,620
biodiversity of the reserve.

47
00:01:28,620 --> 00:01:29,015
Jan:

48
00:01:29,015 --> 00:01:30,200
They certainly are.

49
00:01:30,200 --> 00:01:30,535
Peter:

50
00:01:30,535 --> 00:01:30,871
Oh,

51
00:01:30,871 --> 00:01:35,566
and we're also running a project to identify the different species of butterflies that

52
00:01:35,566 --> 00:01:36,572
visit the reserve.

53
00:01:36,572 --> 00:01:39,590
You might be interested in taking part in that.

54
00:01:39,590 --> 00:01:39,983
Jan:

55
00:01:39,983 --> 00:01:40,375
Sure.

56
00:01:40,375 --> 00:01:45,874
I was involved in something similar where I used to live, counting all the

57
00:01:45,874 --> 00:01:47,052
species of moths.

58
00:01:47,052 --> 00:01:48,230
I'd enjoy that.

59
00:01:48,230 --> 00:01:48,558
Peter:

60
00:01:48,558 --> 00:01:54,792
Another job we're doing at the reserve is replacing the wall on the southern side, between the parking area

61
00:01:54,792 --> 00:01:55,777
and our woodshed.

62
00:01:55,777 --> 00:01:58,730
It was badly damaged in a storm last month.

63
00:01:58,730 --> 00:01:59,690
Jan:

64
00:01:59,690 --> 00:02:00,650
OK.

65
00:02:00,650 --> 00:02:00,924
Peter:

66
00:02:00,924 --> 00:02:04,764
Then as I said, we have a programme of events as well, both at

67
00:02:04,764 --> 00:02:05,313
the weekend,

68
00:02:05,313 --> 00:02:06,410
and during the week.

69
00:02:06,410 --> 00:02:06,799
Jan:

70
00:02:06,799 --> 00:02:07,187
Right.

71
00:02:07,187 --> 00:02:09,520
I presume you have guided walks?

72
00:02:09,520 --> 00:02:14,961
I'd like to get to know the local countryside, as I'm new to the

73
00:02:14,961 --> 00:02:15,350
area.

74
00:02:15,350 --> 00:02:15,654
Peter:

75
00:02:15,654 --> 00:02:16,568
Yes, we do.

76
00:02:16,568 --> 00:02:19,917
The next walk is to Ruston Island, a week on Saturday.

77
00:02:19,917 --> 00:02:23,874
We'll be meeting in the car park at Dunsmore Beach at low tide –

78
00:02:23,874 --> 00:02:28,137
that's when the sands are dry enough for us to walk to the island

79
00:02:28,137 --> 00:02:29,050
without getting wet.

80
00:02:29,050 --> 00:02:29,550
Jan:

81
00:02:29,550 --> 00:02:30,550
Sounds good.

82
00:02:30,550 --> 00:02:30,881
Peter:

83
00:02:30,881 --> 00:02:33,199
The island's a great place to explore.

84
00:02:33,199 --> 00:02:34,193
It's quite small,

85
00:02:34,193 --> 00:02:36,511
and it's got a range of habitats.

86
00:02:36,511 --> 00:02:40,484
It's also an ideal location for seeing seals just off the coast,

87
00:02:40,484 --> 00:02:42,140
or even on the beach.

88
00:02:42,140 --> 00:02:42,601
Jan:

89
00:02:42,601 --> 00:02:43,063
OK.

90
00:02:43,063 --> 00:02:48,600
And is there anything we should bring, like a picnic, for instance?

91
00:02:48,600 --> 00:02:48,957
Peter:

92
00:02:48,957 --> 00:02:52,170
Yes, do bring one, as it's a full-day walk.

93
00:02:52,170 --> 00:02:55,741
And of course it'll be wet walking across and back,

94
00:02:55,741 --> 00:02:58,240
so make sure your boots are waterproof.

95
00:02:58,240 --> 00:02:58,577
Jan:

96
00:02:58,577 --> 00:03:02,961
I must buy a new pair – there's a hole in one of my

97
00:03:02,961 --> 00:03:03,635
current ones!

98
00:03:03,635 --> 00:03:06,670
Well, I'd definitely like to come on the walk.

99
00:03:06,670 --> 00:03:07,079
Peter:

100
00:03:07,079 --> 00:03:07,487
Great.

101
00:03:07,487 --> 00:03:12,800
Then later this month we're having a one-day woodwork session in Hopton Wood.

102
00:03:12,800 --> 00:03:13,176
Jan:

103
00:03:13,176 --> 00:03:15,054
I've never tried that before.

104
00:03:15,054 --> 00:03:18,060
Is it OK for beginners to take part?

105
00:03:18,060 --> 00:03:18,359
Peter:

106
00:03:18,359 --> 00:03:18,658
Definitely.

107
00:03:18,658 --> 00:03:21,348
There'll be a couple of experts leading the session,

108
00:03:21,348 --> 00:03:23,740
and we keep the number of participants down,

109
00:03:23,740 --> 00:03:26,430
so you'll get as much help as you need.

110
00:03:26,430 --> 00:03:26,814
Jan:

111
00:03:26,814 --> 00:03:27,198
Excellent!

112
00:03:27,198 --> 00:03:30,270
I'd love to be able to make chairs.

113
00:03:30,270 --> 00:03:30,614
Peter:

114
00:03:30,614 --> 00:03:33,021
That's probably too ambitious for one day!

115
00:03:33,021 --> 00:03:35,084
You'll be starting with wooden spoons,

116
00:03:35,084 --> 00:03:38,178
and of course learning how to use the tools.

117
00:03:38,178 --> 00:03:41,960
And anything you make is yours to take home with you.

118
00:03:41,960 --> 00:03:42,385
Jan:

119
00:03:42,385 --> 00:03:44,085
That sounds like fun.

120
00:03:44,085 --> 00:03:45,360
When is it?

121
00:03:45,360 --> 00:03:45,765
Peter:

122
00:03:45,765 --> 00:03:49,008
It's on the 17th, from 10 a.m.

123
00:03:49,008 --> 00:03:49,819
until 3.

124
00:03:49,819 --> 00:03:52,656
There's a charge of ￡35, including lunch,

125
00:03:52,656 --> 00:03:56,710
or￡40 if you want to camp in the wood.

126
00:03:56,710 --> 00:03:57,115
Jan:

127
00:03:57,115 --> 00:04:00,760
I should think I'll come home the same day.

128
00:04:00,760 --> 00:04:04,000
Well, I'd certainly like to join the group.
